            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Chemical &amp; Mechanical Stratification of Earth</h3>\n<p>Because direct drilling penetrates only ~12 km (Kola Superdeep Borehole), our understanding of Earth's interior relies on <strong>seismic wave velocity profiling, meteorites, and high-pressure experimental petrology</strong>.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Layer</th>\n      <th>Depth &amp; Density</th>\n      <th>Composition &amp; Rheological State</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Crust</strong></td>\n      <td>Continental: 30-70 km (density 2.7 g/cm³)<br>Oceanic: 5-10 km (density 3.0 g/cm³)</td>\n      <td><strong>Continental Crust (SIAL):</strong> Dominated by silica and alumina; granitic and felsic.<br><strong>Oceanic Crust (SIMA):</strong> Dominated by silica and magnesium; basaltic and mafic. Rigid outer brittle shell.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Mantle</strong></td>\n      <td>30 to 2,900 km (density 3.3 to 5.7 g/cm³)</td>\n      <td>Composes <strong>84% of Earth's volume</strong>. Composed of ultramafic rock (peridotite).<br><em>Asthenosphere (100-400 km):</em> Low-velocity ductile zone (semi-molten plastic layer) driving convective mantle plumes.<br><em>Mesosphere:</em> Lower solid mantle (670-2,900 km).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Core (Barysphere / NIFE)</strong></td>\n      <td>2,900 to 6,371 km (density 9.9 to 13.0+ g/cm³)</td>\n      <td>Dominated by metallic Nickel and Iron (NIFE).<br><strong>Outer Core (2,900-5,150 km):</strong> Liquid molten iron-nickel; geodynamo convective currents generate Earth's geomagnetic field.<br><strong>Inner Core (5,150-6,371 km):</strong> Solid crystalline iron alloy under immense pressure (~3.6 million atm).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>2. The Five Seismic Discontinuities (C-M-R-G-L)</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Conrad Discontinuity:</strong> Transition boundary between the Upper Continental Crust (Granitic) and Lower Crust (Basaltic).</li>\n  <li><strong>Mohorovičić Discontinuity (Moho):</strong> Boundary separating the brittle Crust from the denser peridotitic Mantle (seismic P-wave velocity jumps abruptly from ~6.8 to ~8.1 km/s).</li>\n  <li><strong>Repetti Discontinuity:</strong> Transition boundary between the Upper Mantle and Lower Mantle (~670 km depth).</li>\n  <li><strong>Gutenberg Discontinuity (CMB):</strong> Crucial boundary separating the solid silicate Mantle from the molten metallic Outer Core at <strong>2,900 km depth</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lehmann Discontinuity:</strong> Transition zone between the liquid Outer Core and the solid Inner Core at <strong>5,150 km depth</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Seismic Waves &amp; Shadow Zones</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Primary (P) Waves:</strong> Longitudinal / compressional body waves (particles vibrate parallel to wave propagation). Fastest waves; can travel through <em>solids, liquids, and gases</em>. Refraction at the core-mantle boundary leaves a <strong>P-wave shadow zone between 105° and 145°</strong> from the epicenter.</li>\n  <li><strong>Secondary (S) Waves:</strong> Transverse / shear body waves (particles oscillate perpendicular to wave propagation). Travel <strong>ONLY through solid media</strong>. S-waves are completely absorbed and blocked at the core-mantle boundary (2,900 km), creating a vast <strong>S-wave shadow zone extending beyond 105° all the way to 180°</strong>, proving unequivocally that Earth's outer core is in a liquid/molten state.</li>\n</ul>\n",
