            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Continental Drift Theory (Alfred Wegener, 1912)</h3>\n<p>German meteorologist <strong>Alfred Wegener</strong> postulated that all continents were once assembled into a single supercontinent named <strong>Pangaea</strong> ('All Earth'), surrounded by a mega-ocean named <strong>Panthalassa</strong> ('All Water').</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Breakup Sequence:</strong> Around 200 million years ago (Mesozoic era), Pangaea began splitting into <strong>Laurasia (Angaraland)</strong> in the northern hemisphere and <strong>Gondwanaland</strong> in the southern hemisphere, separated by an east-west trending sea named the <strong>Tethys Sea</strong>. Gondwanaland comprised modern South America, Africa, Peninsular India, Madagascar, Australia, and Antarctica.</li>\n  <li><strong>Geological &amp; Paleontological Evidences:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Jig-Saw Fit:</em> The coastlines of South America and Western Africa match with remarkable mathematical precision across the Atlantic Ocean.</li>\n      <li><em>Paleontological Correlation:</em> Identical fossils of the freshwater reptile <em>Mesosaurus</em> and land reptile <em>Lystrosaurus</em> found on disjointed continents across oceans where they could not have swum.</li>\n      <li><em>Botanical Evidence:</em> <em>Glossopteris</em> fern flora distributed across India, South Africa, Australia, Antarctica, and South America.</li>\n      <li><em>Glacial Tillites:</em> Talchir boulder beds of India match Permo-Carboniferous glacial tillite layers in Madagascar, Africa, Australia, and Antarctica.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Wegener's Flaw:</strong> Wegener attributed drift to <em>tidal forces and pole-fleeing forces (Polflucht)</em>, which physicists proved were hopelessly inadequate to move colossal continents.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Modern Plate Tectonics Paradigm</h3>\n<p>Formulated in the late 1960s based on <strong>Arthur Holmes' Convection Currents</strong>, <strong>Harry Hess' Seafloor Spreading</strong>, and <strong>Paleomagnetic Vine-Matthews anomalies</strong>. The rigid outer <strong>Lithosphere</strong> (~100 km thick) is broken into seven major plates (Pacific, North American, South American, Eurasian, African, Indo-Australian, Antarctic) and numerous minor plates (Nazca, Cocos, Arabian, Caribbean, Philippine).</p>\n\n<h3>3. Types of Plate Margins</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Plate Boundary</th>\n      <th>Mechanical Nature</th>\n      <th>Geological Dynamics &amp; Landform Examples</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Divergent (Constructive)</strong></td>\n      <td>Plates pull apart; basaltic magma ascends from mantle, crystallizing new oceanic crust.</td>\n      <td><strong>Mid-Atlantic Ridge</strong>, East African Great Rift Valley, Red Sea rift. Shallow earthquakes and fissure basalt eruptions.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Convergent (Destructive)</strong></td>\n      <td>Plates collide; denser plate is subducted into mantle at oceanic trench (Benioff zone) or crumples into fold mountains.</td>\n      <td>\n        <em>Ocean-Continent:</em> Andes, Cascades (Volcanic arcs &amp; deep trenches).<br>\n        <em>Ocean-Ocean:</em> Mariana Trench, Japan, Aleutian island arcs.<br>\n        <em>Continent-Continent:</em> <strong>Himalayas</strong>, Alps (Massive orogenic crustal shortening; powerful earthquakes without active volcanism).\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Transform (Conservative)</strong></td>\n      <td>Plates slide horizontally past one another along strike-slip faults; crust is neither created nor destroyed.</td>\n      <td><strong>San Andreas Fault</strong> (California). Severe shallow-focus seismic ruptures with no volcanism.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>4. The Pacific 'Ring of Fire'</h3>\n<p>A 40,000-km horseshoe-shaped subduction perimeter surrounding the Pacific Basin. Characterized by continuous ocean-continent and ocean-ocean convergent boundaries, it accounts for <strong>over 75% of the world's active subaerial volcanoes and ~90% of all global earthquakes</strong>.</p>\n",
