            "id": "m03",
            "title": "Endogenic Forces: Volcanism, Earthquakes, Faults (Horst/Graben) & Folds",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Endogenic Geomorphic Processes</h3>\n<p>Endogenic forces derive their energy from <strong>radioactive decay, primordial gravitational heat, and tidal friction</strong> deep within the mantle. They are classified into:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Diastrophic Forces (Slow Deformations):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Epeirogenic (Continent-Building):</em> Vertical movements causing regional uplift (producing plateaus, marine terraces) or subsidence (submerged coastlines).</li>\n      <li><em>Orogenic (Mountain-Building):</em> Horizontal tangential forces (compression and tension) that deform the crust into fold belts and fault networks.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Catastrophic Movements:</strong> Sudden energy releases manifested as <strong>earthquakes</strong> and <strong>volcanic eruptions</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Structural Mechanics of Folding and Faulting</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Folds (Compressional Strain):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Anticline:</em> Upward convex arch with oldest strata at its core.</li>\n      <li><em>Syncline:</em> Downward concave trough with youngest strata at its core.</li>\n      <li><em>Recumbent Fold &amp; Nappe:</em> When compressional forces are extreme, the axial plane becomes nearly horizontal (recumbent), and further shearing thrusts the rock mass tens of kilometers over foreign strata, creating a <strong>Nappe</strong> (common in the Outer/Lesser Himalayas).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Faults, Horsts, and Grabens:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Normal Fault:</em> Caused by tensional stress; hanging wall moves downward relative to footwall.</li>\n      <li><em>Reverse / Thrust Fault:</em> Caused by compressional stress; hanging wall moves upward over footwall.</li>\n      <li><em>Horst (Block Mountain):</em> An uplifted crustal block between two parallel normal faults (e.g., Vosges in France, Black Forest in Germany, Satpura in India).</li>\n      <li><em>Graben (Rift Valley):</em> A subsided, down-dropped crustal block between two parallel normal faults (e.g., <strong>Narmada and Tapi valleys in India</strong>, East African Rift Valley, Rhine Graben).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Intrusive &amp; Extrusive Volcanic Forms</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Feature</th>\n      <th>Morphological Nature &amp; Formation Mechanics</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Batholith</strong></td>\n      <td>Massive granitic plutonic magma chamber cooled deep within the crust; core of mountain ranges.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Laccolith &amp; Lopolith</strong></td>\n      <td><em>Laccolith:</em> Mushroom/dome-shaped intrusive body arching overlying strata.<br><em>Lopolith:</em> Saucer-shaped basin intrusive body concave upward.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Phacolith</strong></td>\n      <td>Lens-shaped magma mass occupying crest of anticlines or trough of synclines.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Sill vs. Dyke</strong></td>\n      <td><em>Sill:</em> Concordant, horizontal sheet intruded parallel to bedding planes.<br><em>Dyke:</em> Discordant, near-vertical wall-like magma sheet cutting across bedding planes.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Shield Volcano</strong></td>\n      <td>Formed from highly fluid, low-viscosity basaltic lava; low gentle slopes (e.g., Mauna Loa, Hawaii).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Composite Volcano (Stratovolcano)</strong></td>\n      <td>Formed from viscous, silica-rich andesitic/felsic lava alternating with explosive ash/cinders (e.g., Mt. Fuji, Mt. Vesuvius).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Endogenic forces originate within Earth's interior and are categorized into Diastrophic (slow, mountain-building/epeirogenic) and Catastrophic (sudden, earthquakes/volcanoes).",
                "Folds result from horizontal compressional stress, producing upfolds (anticlines), downfolds (synclines), overfolds, and overthrust nappes.",
                "Faults result from tensional (normal faults) or compressional stress (reverse/thrust faults), producing Horst (block mountains) and Graben (rift valleys).",
                "Volcanoes are classified based on magma viscosity into Shield volcanoes (basaltic, low viscosity, effusive) and Composite/Stratovolcanoes (andesitic/felsic, explosive, pyroclastic)."
            ],
