            "id": "m04",
            "title": "Exogenic Geomorphic Processes: Weathering (Physical, Chemical, Biological) & Mass Wasting",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Denudation Continuum</h3>\n<p>While endogenic forces build relief (elevating plateaus and fold mountains), <strong>exogenic forces</strong> ceaselessly degrade, plane down, and level the relief through <strong>denudation</strong> (weathering + mass wasting + erosion + transportation), ultimately seeking an ultimate base level of erosion.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Weathering Mechanisms (In Situ Disintegration)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Physical / Mechanical Weathering:</strong> Disintegration without chemical alteration:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Frost Wedging (Gelifraction):</em> In cold temperate and high-mountain zones, water enters rock fissures and freezes. Since water expands by ~9% upon freezing, it exerts immense bursting pressure (up to 2,000 kg/cm²), shattering rocks into angular scree and talus cones.</li>\n      <li><em>Thermal Expansion &amp; Exfoliation:</em> In hot deserts with high diurnal temperature ranges, differential expansion and contraction of minerals cause rock surfaces to peel off in concentric layers (<strong>exfoliation or onion-skin weathering</strong>), forming rounded exfoliation domes.</li>\n      <li><em>Pressure Release (Unloading / Sheeting):</em> When overlying strata are eroded, underlying plutonic rocks (e.g., granites) expand upward, causing horizontal fracture sheeting.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Chemical Weathering:</strong> Decomposition altering rock mineralogy:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Carbonation:</em> Carbon dioxide dissolved in rainwater forms weak carbonic acid ($H_2CO_3$), which converts insoluble calcium carbonate into soluble calcium bicarbonate:\n        $$CaCO_3 + H_2O + CO_2 \rightleftharpoons Ca(HCO_3)_2$$\n        This drives subterranean limestone dissolution and sinkhole formation.</li>\n      <li><em>Oxidation:</em> Reaction of atmospheric oxygen with iron-bearing minerals (olivine, pyroxene) creating ferric oxides (rusting), weakening the rock matrix.</li>\n      <li><em>Hydration:</em> Chemical addition of water molecules to minerals (e.g., conversion of Anhydrite into Gypsum, or Hematite into Limonite), accompanied by volume expansion.</li>\n      <li><em>Hydrolysis:</em> Reaction with water dissociating into $H^+$ and $OH^-$ ions, decomposing silicate feldspars into kaolinite clay.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Mass Wasting (Gravitational Debris Transfer)</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Mass Wasting Type</th>\n      <th>Rate of Movement</th>\n      <th>Mechanics &amp; Landscape Manifestation</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Soil Creep</strong></td>\n      <td>Extremely slow (mm/yr)</td>\n      <td>Imperceptible downslope creep of soil under gravity; evidenced by tilted utility poles, curved tree trunks, and bent retaining walls.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Solifluction</strong></td>\n      <td>Slow to moderate</td>\n      <td>Occurs in periglacial tundra; surface layer melts in summer and flows as a muddy sludge over impermeable permafrost subsoils, forming solifluction lobes.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Slump (Rotational Slip)</strong></td>\n      <td>Rapid</td>\n      <td>Slipping of rock/regolith along a concave, curved slip plane with backward backward rotation of the slumped block.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Debris Avalanche / Landslide</strong></td>\n      <td>Extremely rapid (catastrophic)</td>\n      <td>Sudden collapse of massive rock masses along steep mountain slopes (e.g., frequent monsoon tragedies in Western Ghats and Himalayas).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Exogenic processes derive energy from solar radiation and gravity, performing denudation (weathering, mass wasting, erosion, and deposition).",
                "Weathering is the in situ (in-place) mechanical breakdown and chemical alteration of rocks without appreciable transport.",
                "Physical/Mechanical weathering includes frost wedging, thermal expansion/exfoliation ('onion skin' weathering), and salt crystal growth.",
                "Chemical weathering involves oxidation, hydration, carbonation (solution in karst), and hydrolysis; it is most intense in warm, humid tropical climates.",
                "Mass wasting is the downslope movement of rock and soil debris under the direct influence of gravity, classified into creep, solifluction, slumps, slides, and debris flows."
            ],
