            "title": "Air Masses, Fronts & Cyclones: Tropical vs Temperate (Extra-Tropical) Cyclones",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Air Masses &amp; Source Regions</h3>\n<p>An <strong>Air Mass</strong> is an extensive body of air whose physical properties (temperature, moisture lapse rate) are horizontally homogeneous over thousands of kilometers. Source regions must be broad, topographically uniform surfaces with calm air (e.g., oceans, ice caps, desert plains).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Classification Scheme (Bergeron):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>cT (Continental Tropical):</em> Hot and dry (e.g., Sahara, Thar).</li>\n      <li><em>mT (Maritime Tropical):</em> Warm and perennially humid (e.g., Caribbean, Bay of Bengal).</li>\n      <li><em>cP (Continental Polar):</em> Frigid and dry (e.g., Siberia, Northern Canada).</li>\n      <li><em>mP (Maritime Polar):</em> Cool and moist (e.g., North Pacific, North Atlantic).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Fronts and Frontogenesis</h3>\n<p>A <strong>Front</strong> is the three-dimensional transitional boundary zone separating two distinct air masses. Because contrasting air masses resist mixing, the lighter (warmer) air mass is invariably forced to ascend over the denser (colder) air mass.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cold Front:</strong> Fast-moving cold air mass wedges beneath and aggressively undercuts a warm air mass. Forces rapid vertical ascent of warm air, creating towering <em>Cumulonimbus</em> clouds, violent squalls, thunderstorms, and hail. Steeper slope (1:50 to 1:100).</li>\n  <li><strong>Warm Front:</strong> Warm air advances and glides gently over retreating cold air. Gentle slope (1:150 to 1:300). Generates a broad sequence of stratiform clouds (Cirrus → Cirrostratus → Altostratus → Nimbostratus) and prolonged, steady drizzle.</li>\n  <li><strong>Occluded Front:</strong> When the faster cold front overtakes the warm front, completely lifting the warm sector off the ground. Marks the terminal phase of an extra-tropical cyclone.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Tropical Cyclones vs. Temperate (Extra-Tropical) Cyclones</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Feature</th>\n      <th>Tropical Cyclones</th>\n      <th>Temperate / Extra-Tropical Cyclones</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Latitudinal Zone</strong></td>\n      <td>Tropical belts between 8° and 20° N &amp; S (strictly absent between 0° and 5° due to negligible Coriolis force).</td>\n      <td>Mid and high latitudes (35° to 65° N &amp; S).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Origin &amp; Energy Mechanism</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Thermal Engine:</strong> Powered exclusively by <em>latent heat of condensation</em> released from vast warm ocean waters (SST &ge; 27°C).</td>\n      <td><strong>Dynamic Frontal Origin:</strong> Driven by horizontal temperature contrasts and baroclinic instability along the Polar Front.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Frontal Structure</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>No Fronts.</strong> Purely symmetrical convective vortex with circular isobars.</td>\n      <td>Possesses distinct Warm and Cold Fronts with V-shaped isobars.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Eye of the Storm</strong></td>\n      <td>Has a distinct, calm, cloudless central <strong>'Eye'</strong> (subsiding dry air) surrounded by an intense <strong>'Eye Wall'</strong>.</td>\n      <td>No central calm eye; entire center is covered by clouds and precipitation.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Movement &amp; Destruction</strong></td>\n      <td>Steered from <strong>East to West</strong> by Trade Winds; dissipates rapidly upon landfall due to cutoff of moisture and surface friction.</td>\n      <td>Steered from <strong>West to East</strong> by the Westerlies; traverses both oceans and landmasses without sudden dissipation.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "An Air Mass is a large body of air covering thousands of square kilometers with uniform temperature and moisture characteristics acquired from its source region.",
                "Fronts are sloping boundary surfaces separating two air masses of differing densities; categorized into Cold Front, Warm Front, Occluded Front, and Stationary Front.",
                "Temperate (Extra-Tropical / Frontal) Cyclones develop in mid-latitudes (35°-65°) along the polar front due to frontogenesis, moving from west to east with the Westerlies.",
                "Tropical Cyclones are thermal low-pressure heat engines powered by latent heat of condensation, forming over warm tropical oceans (sea surface temperature ≥27°C) without fronts."
            ],
