            "title": "World Continents & Landforms: Asia & Africa",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Physiographic Architecture of Asia</h3>\n<p>Asia spans <strong>44.58 million sq km</strong>, encompassing immense climatic and geomorphic extremes:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Pamir Knot ('Roof of the World'):</strong> A complex structural hub in Central Asia from which great mountain chains diverge:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Eastward:</em> <strong>Himalayas</strong>, <strong>Karakoram</strong> (hosting K2 / Godwin-Austen, 8,611 m), and <strong>Kunlun Shan</strong> enclosing the high <strong>Tibetan Plateau</strong> (highest and largest plateau on Earth; average elevation &gt;4,500 m).</li>\n      <li><em>North-Eastward:</em> <strong>Tian Shan</strong> and <strong>Altai Mountains</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Westward:</em> <strong>Hindu Kush</strong>, continuing into the Elburz and Zagros mountains of the Iranian Plateau, converging at the Armenian Knot.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Major Plains and Drainage:</strong> Indo-Gangetic Plains (India/Pakistan), Mesopotamian Plains (Tigris-Euphrates, Iraq), Great Plains of China (Huang He &amp; Yangtze), and Siberian Plains (Ob, Yenisey, Lena flowing into the Arctic Ocean).</li>\n  <li><strong>Extreme Depressions:</strong> The <strong>Dead Sea</strong> shoreline (-430 meters below sea level) on the Jordan-Israel border is Earth's lowest terrestrial elevation. <strong>Lake Baikal</strong> in Siberia is the deepest continental lake (1,642 meters).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Physiographic Architecture of Africa</h3>\n<p>Known as the <strong>'Plateau Continent'</strong>, Africa lacks continuous, continent-spanning fold mountain systems like the Andes or Rockies (apart from the Atlas Mountains in the northwest and Cape Fold mountains in the south).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Great Rift Valley (East African Rift System - EARS):</strong>\n    <p>A 6,400-km active divergent continental rift extending from the Jordan Valley/Red Sea through Ethiopia, Kenya, Tanzania to Mozambique. Along its floor lie deep graben lakes: <strong>Lake Tanganyika</strong> (second deepest in the world, 1,470 m), <strong>Lake Malawi (Nyasa)</strong>, Lake Turkana, and Lake Kivu. (<em>Note:</em> <strong>Lake Victoria</strong>, Africa's largest lake by area, occupies a shallow tectonic sag between the western and eastern rift branches, not the rift graben itself).</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Prominent Peaks and Deserts:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Mount Kilimanjaro (Tanzania):</em> Africa's highest summit (<strong>5,895 meters</strong>); an isolated, dormant volcanic massif capped with glaciers near the equator.</li>\n      <li><em>The Sahara Desert:</em> Earth's largest hot desert (~9.2 million sq km), spanning North Africa from the Atlantic to the Red Sea. Characterized by <em>Ergs</em> (sand seas), <em>Regs</em> (stony plains), and <em>Hamadas</em> (bare rock plateaus).</li>\n      <li><em>Kalahari &amp; Namib Deserts:</em> In Southern Africa; Namib is one of the world's oldest coastal fog deserts.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Hydrography:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Nile River (6,650 km):</em> Longest river on Earth, formed by the union of the <strong>White Nile</strong> (from Lake Victoria) and <strong>Blue Nile</strong> (from Lake Tana, Ethiopia) at Khartoum, Sudan.</li>\n      <li><em>Congo (Zaire) River:</em> World's second largest river by discharge; <strong>crosses the Equator twice</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Zambezi River:</em> Hosts the magnificent <strong>Victoria Falls</strong> (Mosi-oa-Tunya) on the Zambia-Zimbabwe border.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n",
