            "id": "m12",
            "title": "World Continents & Landforms: Europe & Oceania",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. European Physiography</h3>\n<p>Europe (10.18 million sq km) is a western peninsula of the Eurasian landmass, deeply indented by inland seas and gulfs:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Alpine Mountain System:</strong> Young Tertiary fold mountains created by the collision of the African plate with Eurasia:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>The Alps:</em> Spanning France, Switzerland, Italy, Austria, and Germany; highest peak is <strong>Mont Blanc (4,808 m)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>The Pyrenees:</em> Form an imposing natural barrier between France and Spain.</li>\n      <li><em>The Caucasus Mountains:</em> Flank the Black and Caspian seas; host <strong>Mount Elbrus (5,642 m)</strong>, the highest mountain summit in Europe.</li>\n      <li><em>The Ural Mountains:</em> Ancient, heavily eroded Hercynian mountain chain traditionally demarcating the geographical boundary between Europe and Asia.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Great European Plain:</strong> A vast, fertile lowland corridor extending from the Pyrenees across northern France, Belgium, Netherlands, Germany, Poland, and deep into European Russia.</li>\n  <li><strong>Crucial Inland Waterways:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Volga River (3,530 km):</em> Longest river in Europe; drains the Valdai Hills into the landlocked Caspian Sea.</li>\n      <li><em>Danube River (2,850 km):</em> Originates in the Black Forest of Germany and flows through or forms the border of <strong>10 nations</strong> (Austria, Slovakia, Hungary, Croatia, Serbia, Romania, Bulgaria, Moldova, Ukraine) before discharging into the Black Sea.</li>\n      <li><em>Rhine River (1,230 km):</em> Connects the industrial heartland of Germany (Ruhr Basin) to the seaport of Rotterdam on the North Sea.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Australia &amp; Oceania</h3>\n<p>Australia is the smallest continent and the world's lowest, flattest, and driest inhabited continent (often dubbed the <em>'Island Continent'</em>):</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Three Broad Divisions of Australia:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>The Eastern Highlands (Great Dividing Range):</em> Ancient eroded fold mountains running parallel to the east coast from Cape York to Tasmania. Highest peak is <strong>Mount Kosciuszko (2,228 m)</strong> in the Snowy Mountains.</li>\n      <li><em>The Central Lowlands:</em> Encompasses the Murray-Darling river basin, Lake Eyre depression (-15 m, an endorheic salt sink), and the <strong>Great Artesian Basin</strong> (world's largest and deepest groundwater artesian aquifer).</li>\n      <li><em>The Western Plateau:</em> Ancient Precambrian shield occupying two-thirds of the continent, dominated by the Great Sandy, Gibson, and Great Victoria Deserts (<em>The Outback</em>). Famous for monolithic inselbergs like <strong>Uluru (Ayers Rock)</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Great Barrier Reef:</strong>\n    <p>Stretching over <strong>2,300 km</strong> along the northeast coast of Queensland. It is the <strong>largest coral reef system and living organism structure on Earth</strong>, visible from space. Formed by colonies of millions of tiny coral polyps in warm, shallow, clear tropical waters.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>New Zealand &amp; Pacific Subdivisions:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>New Zealand:</em> North Island (active volcanism, geothermal geysers like Rotorua) and South Island (dominated by the glaciated <strong>Southern Alps</strong> with Mt. Cook / Aoraki, 3,724 m).</li>\n      <li><em>Pacific Island Realms:</em> Melanesia (Fiji, Solomon Islands, PNG), Micronesia (Guam, Kiribati, Marshall Islands), and Polynesia (Hawaii, Samoa, Tonga, Easter Island).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n",
