            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. India's Clean Energy Transition</h3>\n<p>At COP26 (Glasgow), India committed to the <strong>'Panchamrit' climate pledges</strong>: achieving <strong>500 GW of non-fossil fuel capacity by 2030</strong>, meeting 50% of energy requirements from renewables, reducing carbon intensity of GDP by 45%, and achieving <strong>Net-Zero emissions by 2070</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Solar Energy Revolution</h3>\n<p>Endowed with ~300 sunny days annually and insolation of 4 to 7 kWh/m²/day, India has experienced exponential solar expansion:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ultra Mega Solar Parks:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Bhadla Solar Park (Rajasthan):</em> Sprawling across 14,000 acres in Jodhpur district; capacity of <strong>2,245 MW</strong>, making it one of the largest solar parks on Earth.</li>\n      <li><em>Pavagada Solar Park / Shakti Sthala (Karnataka):</em> Capacity of <strong>2,050 MW</strong> in Tumakuru district.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The International Solar Alliance (ISA):</strong> Launched jointly by India and France at COP21 in Paris (2015). Headquartered in <strong>Gurugram, Haryana</strong> (the first treaty-based intergovernmental organization headquartered in India). Champions the <em>'One Sun One World One Grid' (OSOWOG)</em> vision.</li>\n  <li><strong>PM Surya Ghar Muft Bijli Yojana (2024):</strong> Massive rooftop solar mission aiming to solarize 10 million households with 300 units of free electricity monthly.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Wind and Nuclear Power</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Wind Energy:</strong> Installed capacity exceeds 44 GW. Dominated by <strong>Gujarat and Tamil Nadu</strong>. The <strong>Muppandal Wind Farm</strong> in Kanyakumari district, Tamil Nadu, is one of the world's largest operational onshore wind farms, harnessing strong winds funneled through the Palghat gap. Offshore wind potential is being explored along Tamil Nadu and Gujarat coasts.</li>\n  <li><strong>India's Three-Stage Nuclear Programme (Dr. Homi J. Bhabha):</strong>\n    <p>Designed to leverage India's modest uranium reserves (1-2% of global supply) but vast <strong>thorium beach sand reserves (~25% of global supply)</strong>:</p>\n    <ol>\n      <li><em>Stage 1 (PHWR):</em> Pressurized Heavy Water Reactors fueled by natural Uranium, producing Plutonium-239 ($^{239}Pu$).</li>\n      <li><em>Stage 2 (FBR):</em> Fast Breeder Reactors fueled by $^{239}Pu$ with a Thorium blanket, breeding fissile Uranium-233 ($^{233}U$). Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor (PFBR, 500 MW) at Kalpakkam, Tamil Nadu.</li>\n      <li><em>Stage 3 (AHWR):</em> Advanced Heavy Water Reactors fueled by Thorium-$^{233}U$ cycles.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <p>Major Nuclear Power Plants: <strong>Tarapur</strong> (Maharashtra - oldest, 1969), <strong>Rawatbhata</strong> (Rajasthan), <strong>Kalpakkam / MAPS</strong> (Tamil Nadu), <strong>Narora</strong> (UP), <strong>Kakrapar</strong> (Gujarat), <strong>Kaiga</strong> (Karnataka), and <strong>Kudankulam</strong> (Tamil Nadu - largest, 2,000+ MW).</p>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. The National Green Hydrogen Mission</h3>\n<p>Notified in 2023 with an outlay of ₹19,744 crore. Mandates production of <strong>5 Million Metric Tonnes (MMT) per annum of Green Hydrogen by 2030</strong> via water electrolysis powered by renewable electricity, supported by the SIGHT (Strategic Interventions for Green Hydrogen Transition) incentive scheme for electrolyzer manufacturing and domestic production.</p>\n",
