            "id": "m20",
            "title": "Population, Demography & Human Geography of India: Census & Tribal Groups",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Demographic Profile of India (Census 2011 Benchmarks)</h3>\n<p>India represents the world's most populous nation today (having surpassed 1.4 billion). Census 2011 benchmarks remain the statutory reference for policy analysis:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Total Population &amp; Growth:</strong> 1,210.9 million (1.21 billion). Decadal growth rate (2001–2011) was <strong>17.7%</strong> (down from 21.5% in 1991–2001), signaling transition toward demographic stabilization.</li>\n  <li><strong>Population Density:</strong> <strong>382 persons per sq km</strong>.\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Highest Density:</em> <strong>Bihar (1,106 persons/sq km)</strong>, followed by West Bengal (1,028) and Kerala (860). National Capital Territory of Delhi records 11,320 persons/sq km.</li>\n      <li><em>Lowest Density:</em> <strong>Arunachal Pradesh (17 persons/sq km)</strong>, followed by Mizoram (52) and Sikkim (86).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Sex Ratio:</strong> <strong>943 females per 1,000 males</strong> (improved from 933 in 2001).\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Highest Sex Ratio:</em> <strong>Kerala (1,084)</strong> and Tamil Nadu (996). Puducherry (1,037) leads among UTs.</li>\n      <li><em>Lowest Sex Ratio:</em> <strong>Haryana (879)</strong> and Jammu &amp; Kashmir (889). Daman &amp; Diu (618) lowest among UTs.</li>\n      <li><em>Child Sex Ratio (0-6 years):</em> Declined from 927 in 2001 to a critical <strong>919 in 2011</strong> (prompting the national <em>Beti Bachao Beti Padhao</em> mission).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Literacy Rate:</strong> National literacy reached <strong>73.0%</strong> (Males: 80.9%, Females: 64.6%, leaving a gender gap of 16.3%). Kerala leads with <strong>94.0%</strong>, while Bihar trails at <strong>61.8%</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Urbanization:</strong> Urban population stood at <strong>31.16%</strong> (377 million), while rural population was <strong>68.84%</strong>. Goa is the most urbanized state (62.2%), while Himachal Pradesh is the least urbanized (10.0%).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Scheduled Tribes &amp; Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs)</h3>\n<p>Scheduled Tribes constitute <strong>8.6% of India's population (104.3 million)</strong>:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Tribal Category</th>\n      <th>Demographic Proportion &amp; Geographical Distribution</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Largest Tribal Groups</strong></td>\n      <td>\n        1. <strong>Bhil:</strong> Largest tribe (~38% of total ST population), spanning Rajasthan, Gujarat, MP, and Maharashtra.<br>\n        2. <strong>Gond:</strong> Second largest, concentrated in central India (MP, Chhattisgarh, Maharashtra, Telangana - historic <em>Gondwana</em>).<br>\n        3. <strong>Santhal:</strong> Third largest, predominant in Jharkhand, West Bengal, and Odisha.\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>State Distribution Extremes</strong></td>\n      <td>\n        • <em>Highest ST Population (Absolute):</em> <strong>Madhya Pradesh</strong> (15.3 million), followed by Maharashtra and Odisha.<br>\n        • <em>Highest ST % of State Area:</em> <strong>Lakshadweep (94.8%)</strong>, <strong>Mizoram (94.4%)</strong>, Nagaland (86.5%), Meghalaya (86.1%).<br>\n        • <em>No ST Population:</em> Punjab, Haryana, Chandigarh, Delhi, and Puducherry.\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs)</strong></td>\n      <td>\n        <strong>75 designated tribes</strong> characterized by pre-agricultural level of technology, stagnant/declining population, extremely low literacy, and subsistence economy.<br>\n        • <em>Odisha</em> hosts the largest number of PVTGs (13 groups).<br>\n        • <em>Andaman &amp; Nicobar Groups:</em> <strong>Great Andamanese, Onge, Jarawa, Sentinelese</strong> (Negrito hunter-gatherers) and <strong>Shompen</strong> (Mongoloid).<br>\n        • Supported by the <strong>PM-JANMAN</strong> (Pradhan Mantri Janjati Adivasi Nyaya Maha Abhiyan, 2023).\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n",
